 "rulebook": "No official rule book exists: the sweeper is a party and amusement game, not a sport. The closest real device is Galaxy Multi Rides' 'Meltdown' (US Meltdown operator manual: an eight-player game, each player standing on a podium round a motion base whose foam booms spin round, 'the aim of the game is to either duck under, or jump over the boom as it spins around'; players must not grab, hold or ride the booms; a player who is hit is out; one 1 hp spin motor on a speed dial, at most 15.82 rpm; foam boom attachments 220 lb for the two booms; riders at least 48 in (1.22 m) tall and at most 200 lb), the same game as the 'Sweeper' of the TV show Wipeout. Its rules as played: stay on your podium, jump the low boom each time it comes round, the last player not hit wins",
 "deviations": [
  "the booms: Meltdown's dual attachment has two foam booms; here there is only the low boom you jump (the duck boom is left off: this family tests the jump), so the rotor is that one boom, 50.0 kg, half of the attachment's 220 lb",
  "the dimensions: the manual gives none of the boom's height, length or thickness, nor of the podiums. The boom's axis is 0.2 m above the podium's top, the mid-shin of a rider of the minimum height (1.22 m), its foam 0.2 m thick, reaching 4.0 m from the axis over the podiums of a 30 ft bed, whose centres are 3.2 m from the axis; each podium a 0.9 m disc 0.4 m high (reasonable sizes, not published ones)",
  "the podium and the bed: the inflatable podium is a rigid platform and the inflatable bed a firm floor (neither's give is modelled; touching the bed is out anyway)",
  "the speed: the operator's dial is set to a steady 8.0 rpm (about half the machine's top speed), already turning at the start; the machine's program, which changes speed, is not used",
  "the count: one player, and a run of 3 jumps in a row with no hit is the success (a round number: the game has no count, its last player standing wins); after the last jump the robot must stand still for 1.0 s on its podium before the boom comes round again",
  "a hit is any touch of the boom on the robot (in the game a light touch may leave a player standing); a jump is a pass of the boom under both feet while nothing of the robot touches anything",
  "the motor: the 1 hp drive is a speed loop with a torque limit of 450.0 N m at the boom (1 hp at the top speed of 15.82 rpm, through the gearbox), no gearbox backlash or compliance; the foam's contact is a critically damped spring of 0.02 s time constant (an estimate for a soft polyurethane pad)"
 ],
 "texts": {
  "sentence": "Jump the sweeper: standing on your podium, jump over the padded boom each time it sweeps round, 3 times in a row without being hit, then stand still on the podium.",
  "rules": "You stand on a round podium, 0.9 m across and 0.4 m high, whose centre is 3.2 m from the axis of the motion base, facing the base. A foam boom 0.2 m thick, its axis 0.2 m above the podium's top, turns round the base counter-clockwise (seen from above) at 8.0 revolutions per minute, driven by a motor of at most 450.0 N m; it reaches 4.0 m from the axis, over the whole podium, and comes round once every 7.5 s. It is already turning at the start. A jump counts when the boom passes under both your feet while nothing of you touches anything (at the moment it passes each foot). You succeed when, after 3 counted jumps, your first contact is your feet on the podium and you then stand still for 1.0 s (only your feet on the podium, your torso within about 20 degrees of vertical, your pelvis slower than 0.35 m/s), before the boom comes round again. You fail at once if the boom touches any part of you (a hit; grabbing or holding it is a hit too), if any part of you touches the bed (the floor round the podium) or the motion base, if the boom passes through a foot that is not above it, on a fall (your pelvis less than 0.35 m above the podium's top, or any part of you other than your feet on the podium), on a landing after the last jump that is not on the feet, or after 30 s.",
  "venue": "A Meltdown sweeper on a level bed: the motion base, a column 0.4 m in radius, stands 3.2 m in front of you (you face it); you stand in the middle of your podium, its top 0.4 m above the bed. The boom sticks out from the base at shin height and turns counter-clockwise seen from above: it comes at you from your left. At the start it points 200.0 degrees of its turn before your podium (on the far side of the base), turning; it will be over your podium in about 4.17 s.",
  "difficulty": "Neither built-in gait can jump: the robot must jump with its feet above a boom almost as high as its knees, at the moment a fast boom arrives, and land on a small podium in balance, three times. Each landing has to be absorbed and the robot re-centred on the podium before the boom comes round again."
 },
